                Using Logical Disk Mirroring for High Availability
                ==================================================

                If your system uses model 6236- 6239-, or 6580-class disks
		you can use hardware mirroring to make the data on your
	|	system highly available.  Otherwise, with any disks of any
	|	type, you can use software mirroring.  Logical disk mirroring
	|	makes this data highly available by allowing controller
	|	microcode or AOS/VS II itself to maintain two logically
		identical copies of a mirrored LD.  Should a copy of this
                LDU go out of service (for backup) or go bad (suffer a
		hardware error), AOS/VS II will continue to run on the good
		image.

                Logical disk mirroring may or may not have a performance cost.
                Because each LDU image is logically -- not physically --
                identical to the other, the system must write all data
                separately to each LD image.  However, the system also
                optimizes each read by reading from the disk where the
		read/write head is closer to the data.  Since the ratio of
		reads to writes is specific to each application, there is no
		way to figure the performance cost of logical disk mirroring.

	|	For more information about mirroring, see the manual
	|	"Managing AOS/VS II."


                What Mirroring Requires
                -----------------------

                Hardware and software logical disk mirroring requires that
		you make the following the same for each LD image:

                o  LD name

                o  Bad block table entries

                o  Sizes and addresses of the remap and diagnostic areas

                The LD unique IDs, however, must be different.

		Again, only hardware mirroring requires that the images
		be on the models listed above.


