                Starting Mirroring
                ------------------

                The way that you start mirroring depends on the state of the
                LD images.  The LD images can be in either of two states:
                they can be "synchronized" -- the images are the same, or
                "unsynchronized" -- the images differ.


                Starting a Mirror Using Synchronized LDs
                ----------------------------------------

                If the LD images are synchronized, you start mirroring by
                issuing the ?XINIT system call.  Should you specify
                unsynchronized LD images in the ?XINIT system call packet,
                AOS/VS II will return an error.


                Starting a Mirror Using Unsynchronized LDs
                ------------------------------------------

                If the LD images are unsynchronized, you start mirroring in
                two steps.  The first step is to issue the ?XINIT system call,
                specifying the "override error conditions" option, and
                initialize the "good" LD image.  We describe how to determine
                which LD image is good when we describe how to get status
                information about LD images later in this chapter.

                The second step is to synchronize the uninitialized LD image
                with the initialized LD image.  To synchronize the LD images,
                you must issue the ?MIRROR system call.

                The synchronization of LD images may take only a few minutes
                or may take much longer, depending on the amount of data
                that AOS/VS II must copy from one image to the other.
                Synchronization can take up to two hours for a 354 Mbyte
                disk, and up to three-and-a half hours for a 592 Mbyte disk.
	|	With Model 6580 disks, however, synchronization using hard-
	|	ware mirroring is only minutes.  While synchronization is
		going on, normal system activity does too: AOS/VS II writes
		to both disks, but reads from only the "good" disk.


