        On page 9-2, replace the section Why Use Process Connections? with
	the following section.
		
			Why Use Process Connections?

		When you create a customer/server relationship between
		processes, you can use the server process to perform cerain
		functions on behalf of its customer processes.  Typically, a
		server process performs general routines that customer
		processes can access.  For example, you can create a server
		process to build files or perform I/O.

		Connection management allows servers to move bytes to and from
		their customers' buffers.

	|	Connection management is also useful for double process
	|	connections (peer-to-peer applications) which communicate via
	|	the IPC system calls.  Connection management can ensure global
	|	port numbers remain unique until the connection is explicitly
	|	broken.  With the connection management obituary messages,
	|	processes can be notified when peer processes have terminated.
	|	For more information on IPC calls, see Chapter 8.


